===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A flat curved [[airfoil]] that spins about an axis perpendicular to the direction of flight, originally used in various parts of the world as a hunting weapon or, in returnable types, for sports or training.
#: {{syn|en|kylie}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1884|author=w:Andrew Lang|chapter=Star Myths|title=[[s:Custom and Myth/Star Myths|Custom and Myth]]
|passage=Some resemblance to terrestrial things, it is true, everyone can behold in the heavens. ''Corona'', for example, is like a crown, or, as the Australian black fellows know, it is like a '''boomerang''', and we can understand why they give it the name of that curious curved missile.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1897|author=Warren Bert Kimberly|title=s:History of West Australia|passage=With '''boomerang''' and spear they hunted the kangaroo and emu, and fought their battles beneath the eucalyptus forests; their minds, fresh, untroubled, contented, oblivious alike of noble ideals and philosophic principles.}}
#* '''1961''', {{w|Charlie Drake}}, song, ''My '''Boomerang''' Won't Come Back'',
#*: "Don't worry, boy, I know the trick, / And to you I'm gonna show it. / If you want your '''boomerang''' to come back, / Well first you've got to... throw it."
# A [[breakdancing]] [[move]] in which the performer walks on their hands while keeping the [[leg]]s raised off the ground.
# {{lb|en|Australian rules football|rugby}} A [[boomerang kick]].
# {{lb|en|theatre}} A device for changing the color of a [[followspot]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=Steven Louis Shelley|title=A Practical Guide to Stage Lighting
|passage=Roll: A live action changing from one color to another. Typically requires dexterity to "crossfade" between two colors in the '''boomerang''' with one hand while following with the other.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=John Holloway|title=Illustrated Theatre Production Guide|page=144
|passage=If you are spot one you should push down on the first lever on the '''boomerang''' to load frame one. Spot two should do the same with frame five. On the go, both spot ops should run their dowser levers to bring the light up on the target specified by the cue caller.}}
# {{lb|en|{{w|Royal Air Force|RAF}} slang}} The [[early]] [[return]] of an [[aircraft]] whose [[mission]] was [[aborted]], often due to [[technical]] [[failure]]s.
#* {{seeCites|en}}
# A [[cocktail]] made with [[rye]] [[whiskey]] and Swedish [[punsch]].

